## Command Design
- Keep commands focused on a single task or purpose
- Use Laravel's built-in command structure and inheritance
- Follow naming conventions for commands (verb:noun)
- Design commands to be idempotent when possible
- Create clear, descriptive command names and signatures
- Group related commands under namespaces

## Command Implementation
- Use argument and option descriptions to document usage
- Implement proper input validation in commands
- Use dependency injection in the handle method
- Follow best practices for verbosity levels
- Implement progress bars for long-running operations
- Use tables, info, and other output helpers for readable results

## Error Handling
- Implement proper error handling in commands
- Use try/catch blocks for expected exceptions
- Return appropriate exit codes based on command results
- Log errors appropriately for debugging
- Provide helpful error messages to users
- Include verbosity options for detailed error information

## Performance
- Use Laravel's queue system for long-running commands
- Implement batching for processing large datasets
- Profile command performance for optimization
- Use chunk methods when dealing with large collections
- Implement timeout handling for external dependencies
- Avoid memory leaks in long-running commands

## Documentation & Testing
- Document commands with clear descriptions and signatures
- Include example usage in command help text
- Create dedicated test cases for Artisan commands
- Test commands with varying input scenarios
- Test error cases and edge conditions
- Include commands in your CI/CD pipeline tests

## Maintenance
- Test commands thoroughly before deployment
- Refactor commands as application requirements change
- Keep command dependencies minimal and explicit
- Use service classes for complex business logic
- Implement versioning for breaking changes in commands
- Schedule regular maintenance commands using Laravel's scheduler
